<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wang Jingwei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Wang Zhaoming</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wang Jingwei was a leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Chinese Nationalist Party</a>, a close associate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> and a rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>. In 1938 he broke with Chiang and sought peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and in 1940 he became head of a Japanese-sponsored Reorganised National Government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> that absorbed the earlier collaborationist regimes. He died in Japan in 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wang-jingwei.jpg" alt="Wang Jingwei" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B1%AA%E7%B2%BE%E8%A1%9B%E7%85%A7%E7%89%87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wang Zhaoming was born in Sanshui, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> origin, and passed the county-level imperial examination for the xiucai degree in 1902. In 1903 he went to study at Hosei University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on a Qing government scholarship. In 1905 he met the exiled revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongmenghui">Tongmenghui</a> in Tokyo. He gained attention as a polemicist for its journal People's News, notably in debates with Liang Qichao, who advocated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>. His pen name Wang Jingwei, adopted in 1905, came from the mythical jingwei bird that tries to fill the ocean with twigs and pebbles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1910 Wang and others in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongmenghui">Tongmenghui</a> cell planted a bomb meant for the regent, Prince Chun. It was discovered, and he was arrested and sentenced to life imprisonment, but was released after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang-uprising">Wuchang Uprising</a>. After Sun's death in 1925 he became the first president of the Nationalist government, then controlling only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, and adhered to Sun's policy of cooperation with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>. He became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>'s principal rival in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>.</p><p>In 1927 he led the left-wing government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a> against Chiang's at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, but in the July 15 Incident he followed Chiang in purging the Communists. In 1930 he joined a coalition against Chiang that was defeated in the Central Plains War. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukden-incident">Mukden Incident</a> the two reached an accommodation, and Wang served as premier while Chiang commanded the army.</p><p>During the Second Sino-Japanese War Wang at first advocated resistance while negotiating, and from 1937 held secret talks with Konoe Fumimaro. In December 1938 he left the wartime capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> and called for a peace settlement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, for which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> expelled him. On 30 March 1940 he became head of the Reorganized National Government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, which nominally governed Japanese-occupied China outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. It copied the structure of the Republic of China's government and used its blue sky and white sun flag.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In March 1944 Wang went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> for treatment of a wound from an assassination attempt in 1935, and he died at the Nagoya Imperial University Hospital on 10 November 1944, less than a year before Japan's surrender. A testament attributed to him, published in the 1960s, claims to record his deathbed wishes; its authenticity remains disputed. Both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> denounced him as hanjian, a traitor to the Chinese people. He also wrote poetry, and his poems and a literary column on the South Society have been published.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wang Jingwei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Wang Jingwei</b>, a close associate of Sun Yat-sen and rival of Chiang Kai-shek, believed China could not win the war against Japan.</blockquote><a href="/state/wang-jingwei-regime">Wang Jingwei regime</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sanshui District, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.24,112.9&amp;year=1922" aria-label="Sanshui District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7564 1307 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Wang Jingwei"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8014 1577h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8014 1577h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23.24,112.9&amp;year=1922">What was here in 1922? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
